<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drukpa Kagyu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Drukpa lineage</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Drukpa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu</a>, or Drukpa lineage, is a school of the Kagyu tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> founded in the late 12th century by Tsangpa Gyare, named after the Druk monastery he founded near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>. In the 17th century the Drukpa lama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Ngawang Namgyal</a> fled to Bhutan and unified the country. The school has since been Bhutan's state religion, and the country's own name, Druk Yul, derives from it.</p></div>
